WEBVTT

cue0143219
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.200
Now, and the topic, first of all, is subject matter.

cue0143220
00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:13.960
Subject matter is religion and science.

cue0143221
00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:14.960
This is just a chapter.

cue0143222
00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:20.000
And this is the longest chapter, it is about 80 pages.

cue0143223
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:28.000
This chapter is more than 80 pages, 90 pages, and very dense pages.

cue0143224
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:34.520
It's a book, could be, I mean, if a person would write with all the details, this makes

cue0143225
00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:36.520
a thick book.

cue0143226
00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:46.400
Normally, or in certain circles, people assume that there are two ways, it's either the way

cue0143227
00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:58.400
of religion, faith, devotion, prophecy, or the way of science.

cue0143228
00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:12.120
And there are two ways, and we have to make a choice.

cue0143229
00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:18.519
It's either the way of science, or the way of religion.

cue0143230
00:01:18.519 --> 00:01:23.639
Each way is incompatible with the other.

cue0143231
00:01:23.639 --> 00:01:32.040
The purpose of Rabbi David Nieto is, in this chapter, is to show that this notion is false.

cue0143232
00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:38.080
There is no such a view in Judaism.

cue0143233
00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:42.800
And basically, what he shows is the following.

cue0143234
00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:46.519
Yes, there are some statements in the Talmud.

cue0143235
00:01:46.519 --> 00:01:55.639
But those statements are either deliberately, or out of ignorance, misinterpreted.

cue0143236
00:01:55.639 --> 00:02:00.599
There's one statement which everybody quotes and says,

cue0143237
00:02:00.599 --> 00:02:15.600
The tears of the children don't bother me at all, let them cry, let them play, let them jump, let them do whatever they want.

cue0143238
00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:18.600
Can I tell you a story about the Bishaka Barmanel?

cue0143239
00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:23.600
Very briefly.

cue0143240
00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:34.600
And every Socrates in Spain wanted to convince the Bishaka Barmanel of the superiority of Christianity.

cue0143241
00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:37.600
And he says, I'm going to bring you a proof.

cue0143242
00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:43.600
The proof is, go to a synagogue, to a Betachnesset.

cue0143243
00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:49.600
And you see people talking, fighting, jumping, arguing.

cue0143244
00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:57.600
Go to a church. Everybody is silent, quiet, respect.

cue0143245
00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:06.600
That in itself shows you, the aristocrats said to the Bishaka Barmanel, of the superiority of Christianity over Judaism.

cue0143246
00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:09.600
Bishaka Barmanel starts talking about other subjects.

cue0143247
00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:12.600
He began to digress.

cue0143248
00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:15.600
And through the digression, he says, you know, you have children.

cue0143249
00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:19.600
And how many grandchildren? I don't know. Ten grandchildren.

cue0143250
00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:23.600
And he says, what happens when the grandchildren come on Sunday for lunch?

cue0143251
00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:29.600
Oh, don't tell me. Start fighting, jumping, noise, so on.

cue0143252
00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:34.600
He says, you know, I remember a couple of months ago, his mother died.

cue0143253
00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:37.600
The mother of this guy.

cue0143254
00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:39.600
When they went to the velorio.

cue0143255
00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:42.600
You know where the velorio is, yes?

cue0143256
00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:45.600
Awakening.

cue0143257
00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:48.600
Should the children behave? Oh, yes.

cue0143258
00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:52.600
Respect for the dead.

cue0143259
00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:55.600
So Bishaka Barmanel says, this is my answer.

cue0143260
00:03:55.600 --> 00:04:02.600
When children come to the synagogue, it is the house of the father.

cue0143261
00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:04.600
And the father is alive and well.

cue0143262
00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:11.600
And they jump, and they fight, and they laugh, and they talk, because they are in their father's home.

cue0143263
00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:14.600
And you, is dead, is a velorio.

cue0143264
00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:18.600
And that's why everybody behaves accordingly.

cue0143265
00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:21.600
So, if grown-ups speak, sometimes it's not.

cue0143266
00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:25.600
But if children, they jump in with, let them jump.

cue0143267
00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:27.600
The most important thing, they should not come to the synagogue.

cue0143268
00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:29.600
That's my philosophy. Anyhow.

cue0143269
00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:33.600
So, there is a passage in the Talmud.

cue0143270
00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:36.600
I wrote extensively about the precise meaning of this passage.

cue0143271
00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:37.600
That says,

cue0143272
00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:49.600
He who reads books, which are extraneous, outside books,

cue0143273
00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:52.600
he has no portion in the world to come.

cue0143274
00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:59.600
So, many people say, oh, what does it mean, extraneous books?

cue0143275
00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:04.600
It doesn't mean books of science, of philosophy.

cue0143276
00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:11.600
And on this basis, they prohibit the community to read scientific books.

cue0143277
00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:21.600
David Nieto proves that the Seferim Hessonim have nothing to do with philosophy, with science.

cue0143278
00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:27.600
In an article that I wrote, I showed two very important points.

cue0143279
00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:31.600
Number one, the word Hessonim means the Christian scripture.

cue0143280
00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:37.600
And Kore means reading this scripture with Ta'amim.

cue0143281
00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:39.600
And it makes sense.

cue0143282
00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:43.600
If you take a book from the Christian scripture, yes?

cue0143283
00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:45.600
One of the Gospels.

cue0143284
00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:51.600
And you read it with the same Ta'amim that we use for reading the Tora.

cue0143285
00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:57.600
You are saying that this and the Tora, they have the same level.

cue0143286
00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:00.600
That is what the Ḥakhamim prohibit.

cue0143287
00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:10.600
In no way, in no way, or in no place, the Ḥakhamim prohibit to read science.

cue0143288
00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:12.600
And he brings many proofs.

cue0143289
00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:21.600
But among the many proofs that he brings, are two that for me, personally, have a lot of significance.

cue0143290
00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:27.600
Rambam, my manners, that's it, based upon the Gemaraʾ.

cue0143291
00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:33.600
And Rabbi Joseph Karol, in Shohan Aruch, also based on the Gemaraʾ,

cue0143292
00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:37.600
make a very interesting decision.

cue0143293
00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:38.600
Pesaḥ.

cue0143294
00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:40.600
It is like this.

cue0143295
00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:45.600
In Shabbat, you should not read scientific books.

cue0143296
00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:48.600
But only the Hebrew Tora.

cue0143297
00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:50.600
So Rabbi David Nieto says,

cue0143298
00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:55.600
By the fact that it tells you, don't read scientific books in Shabbat,

cue0143299
00:06:55.600 --> 00:07:00.600
what does it mean, the rest of the week, read it.

cue0143300
00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:07.600
So now, it says, there are no source.

cue0143301
00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:11.600
And there aren't any source in the entire rabbinic literature,

cue0143302
00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:19.600
that in any way, prohibits someone to study science.

cue0143303
00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:21.600
But it goes more than this.

cue0143304
00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:27.600
It says, if you for instance, read the book of Job,

cue0143305
00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:32.600
from chapter 35 to chapter 42,

cue0143306
00:07:32.600 --> 00:07:35.600
seven large chapters,

cue0143307
00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:38.600
you see in the book of Job,

cue0143308
00:07:38.600 --> 00:07:43.600
scientific description of natural phenomena.

cue0143309
00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:46.600
It is a scientific description.

cue0143310
00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:50.600
And yes, anybody who would open the book of Job,

cue0143311
00:07:50.600 --> 00:07:54.600
he would see the 35 to 42,

cue0143312
00:07:54.600 --> 00:08:01.600
how there is a description, scientific objective of nature.

cue0143313
00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:05.600
But more than this, you have in the Psalms.

cue0143314
00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:08.600
For instance, I love that.

cue0143315
00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:11.600
When David the King sees the stars and so on,

cue0143316
00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:12.600
He says,

cue0143317
00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:23.600
Here you have King David contemplating nature.

cue0143318
00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:26.600
And from the majesty of nature,

cue0143319
00:08:26.600 --> 00:08:34.599
he comes to awe, to fear, and knowledge about the God.

cue0143320
00:08:34.599 --> 00:08:37.599
But more than this,

cue0143321
00:08:38.599 --> 00:08:44.599
Today, this is a story or a tradition among Jews.

cue0143322
00:08:44.599 --> 00:08:50.599
Today, that tradition is confirmed by scientific research.

cue0143323
00:08:50.599 --> 00:08:54.599
When you look into the documentation,

cue0143324
00:08:54.599 --> 00:08:57.599
which was discovered in the last hundred years,

cue0143325
00:08:57.599 --> 00:09:00.599
coming from Babylonia,

cue0143326
00:09:00.599 --> 00:09:03.599
you see that in Babylonia,

cue0143327
00:09:03.599 --> 00:09:07.599
more or less at the time of ʾAbraham ʾAḇinu,

cue0143328
00:09:07.599 --> 00:09:13.599
they had a very advanced knowledge of mathematics.

cue0143329
00:09:13.599 --> 00:09:19.599
Neubauer, who wrote ancient science in Babylonia,

cue0143330
00:09:19.599 --> 00:09:23.599
it's a paperback, you could find it, I mean, you could buy it in any store.

cue0143331
00:09:23.599 --> 00:09:25.599
He says, he comes to the conclusion,

cue0143332
00:09:25.599 --> 00:09:27.599
his field is history of science, ancient science.

cue0143333
00:09:27.599 --> 00:09:32.599
That the mathematics known in Babylonia,

cue0143334
00:09:32.599 --> 00:09:36.599
about 1800 BC,

cue0143335
00:09:36.599 --> 00:09:45.599
it's comparable to what Europe knew of mathematics right before Newton.

cue0143336
00:09:45.599 --> 00:09:48.599
But there's something more amazing.

cue0143337
00:09:48.599 --> 00:09:55.599
Scientists also have shown that the astronomers in Babylonia,

cue0143338
00:09:55.599 --> 00:10:01.599
they knew the different phases of Venus.

cue0143339
00:10:01.599 --> 00:10:07.599
Something that the naked eye doesn't show you.

cue0143340
00:10:07.599 --> 00:10:13.599
Apparently, they knew the different phases of Venus through mathematical calculations.

cue0143341
00:10:13.599 --> 00:10:19.599
They knew about the moons of Jupiter.

cue0143342
00:10:19.599 --> 00:10:22.599
We don't know how they knew that.

cue0143343
00:10:22.599 --> 00:10:27.599
Now, in view of what modern research has shown,

cue0143344
00:10:27.599 --> 00:10:32.599
the story of the tradition of the Jews, an old tradition,

cue0143345
00:10:32.599 --> 00:10:35.599
I found it already in a book written in Greek,

cue0143346
00:10:35.599 --> 00:10:42.599
by a Jewish historian who was in Egypt second century BC.

cue0143347
00:10:42.599 --> 00:10:45.599
He says, his name is Erpel Omeos,

cue0143348
00:10:45.599 --> 00:10:53.599
and he says that ʾAbraham ʾAḇinu was the great scientist of Babylonia,

cue0143349
00:10:53.599 --> 00:10:57.599
who knew mathematics and astronomy.

cue0143350
00:10:57.599 --> 00:10:59.599
Through his mathematics and astronomy,

cue0143351
00:10:59.599 --> 00:11:04.599
he came to realize that there's only one creator in the universe,

cue0143352
00:11:04.599 --> 00:11:08.599
and he is the one who came down to Egypt,

cue0143353
00:11:08.599 --> 00:11:14.599
and introduced mathematics, geometry, and astronomy,

cue0143354
00:11:14.599 --> 00:11:16.599
to Egypt.

cue0143355
00:11:16.599 --> 00:11:23.599
So, not only that the Tora does not oppose scientific studies,

cue0143356
00:11:23.599 --> 00:11:29.599
moreover, the Jews, beginning with ʾAbraham ʾAḇinu,

cue0143357
00:11:29.599 --> 00:11:35.599
were the ones who introduced and developed,

cue0143358
00:11:35.599 --> 00:11:40.599
what may be properly described as scientific methodology,

cue0143359
00:11:41.599 --> 00:11:44.599
the scientific outlook.

cue0143360
00:11:44.599 --> 00:11:47.599
And it makes sense, it makes sense.

cue0143361
00:11:47.599 --> 00:11:52.599
If you believe that there is only one God who created everything,

cue0143362
00:11:52.599 --> 00:11:58.599
then somehow it has to be a relationship between an amoeba,

cue0143363
00:11:58.599 --> 00:12:01.599
which is the smallest form of life,

cue0143364
00:12:01.599 --> 00:12:04.599
and a dinosaur.

cue0143365
00:12:04.599 --> 00:12:06.599
Because everything was created by one God.

cue0143366
00:12:06.599 --> 00:12:13.599
It has to be some relationship between a grain of salt,

cue0143367
00:12:13.599 --> 00:12:17.599
and the largest body in the skies.

cue0143368
00:12:17.599 --> 00:12:23.599
The whole notion that there is one God who made everything,

cue0143369
00:12:23.599 --> 00:12:27.599
it already imposes on us,

cue0143370
00:12:27.599 --> 00:12:30.599
the uniformity of the laws of nature.

cue0143371
00:12:30.599 --> 00:12:36.599
That everything must correlate with something else.

cue0143372
00:12:36.599 --> 00:12:40.599
But there is more than that.

cue0143373
00:12:40.599 --> 00:12:44.599
Before we're going to go into the Talmud,

cue0143374
00:12:44.599 --> 00:12:46.599
I want to tell you,

cue0143375
00:12:46.599 --> 00:12:49.599
this comes also from Rabbi David Nieto,

cue0143376
00:12:49.599 --> 00:12:55.599
about a gentleman by the name of Abraham Cohen de Herrera.

cue0143377
00:12:55.599 --> 00:12:59.599
Abraham Cohen de Herrera, 17th century, 1600s,

cue0143378
00:12:59.599 --> 00:13:04.599
he was a captain in the Spanish army.

cue0143379
00:13:04.599 --> 00:13:07.599
Somehow he had to come to Brussels,

cue0143380
00:13:07.599 --> 00:13:11.599
military and diplomatic business,

cue0143381
00:13:11.599 --> 00:13:14.599
and he comes to Brussels, he escapes from Brussels,

cue0143382
00:13:14.599 --> 00:13:21.599
and goes to Amsterdam, and openly converts back to Judaism.

cue0143383
00:13:21.599 --> 00:13:23.599
Abraham Cohen de Herrera,

cue0143384
00:13:23.599 --> 00:13:25.599
when he was living as a Christian,

cue0143385
00:13:25.599 --> 00:13:27.599
obviously his name was Abraham de Herrera,

cue0143386
00:13:27.599 --> 00:13:29.599
I don't remember, not Abraham,

cue0143387
00:13:29.599 --> 00:13:31.599
the first name probably was Alberto, I suppose too,

cue0143388
00:13:31.599 --> 00:13:34.599
but Herrera, when he converts back to Judaism.

cue0143389
00:13:34.599 --> 00:13:39.599
And this man, he's fascinated by one subject,

cue0143390
00:13:39.599 --> 00:13:44.599
qabbala, mysticism, he wants qabbala.

cue0143391
00:13:44.599 --> 00:13:48.599
And he studies, and for the Sephardim,

cue0143392
00:13:48.599 --> 00:13:50.599
this is the maximum authority,

cue0143393
00:13:50.599 --> 00:13:54.599
there is no maximum authority in qabbala,

cue0143394
00:13:54.599 --> 00:13:56.599
nobody is greater than Ha'ari.

cue0143395
00:13:56.599 --> 00:13:58.599
But Is'haq de Luria,

cue0143396
00:13:58.599 --> 00:14:01.599
for the Sephardim, ask anybody from Morocco,

cue0143397
00:14:01.599 --> 00:14:05.599
from Turkey, from Syria, from Ha'ari,

cue0143398
00:14:05.599 --> 00:14:08.599
that's maximum, that's for Sephardim and Sephardim.

cue0143399
00:14:08.599 --> 00:14:12.599
And he had one student,

cue0143400
00:14:12.599 --> 00:14:17.599
Israel Strugo, we have the family, Strugo.

cue0143401
00:14:17.599 --> 00:14:19.599
You have in Morocco?

cue0143402
00:14:19.599 --> 00:14:26.599
And this man, Abraham Cohen de Herrera,

cue0143403
00:14:26.599 --> 00:14:32.599
becomes a pupil of a pupil of Ha'ari, of Strugo.

cue0143404
00:14:32.599 --> 00:14:34.599
And after he becomes a pupil,

cue0143405
00:14:34.599 --> 00:14:36.599
he understands qabbala,

cue0143406
00:14:36.599 --> 00:14:41.599
he writes a book, in Spanish,

cue0143407
00:14:41.599 --> 00:14:44.599
Las Puertas del Cielo,

cue0143408
00:14:44.599 --> 00:14:46.599
The Gates of Heaven.

cue0143409
00:14:46.599 --> 00:14:53.599
And it is a mystical view of Judaism,

cue0143410
00:14:53.599 --> 00:14:55.599
written in Spanish.

cue0143411
00:14:55.599 --> 00:14:59.599
Maybe some of you, I'm hinting at the Rabbi,

cue0143412
00:14:59.599 --> 00:15:01.599
who knows Spanish, could edit this book

cue0143413
00:15:01.599 --> 00:15:03.599
for the rest of the public.

cue0143414
00:15:03.599 --> 00:15:04.599
Think about it.

cue0143415
00:15:04.599 --> 00:15:06.599
We have a copy of the manuscript

cue0143416
00:15:06.599 --> 00:15:08.599
at Columbia University.

cue0143417
00:15:08.599 --> 00:15:11.599
Las Puertas del Cielo.

cue0143418
00:15:12.599 --> 00:15:14.599
And David Arminiento says,

cue0143419
00:15:14.599 --> 00:15:16.599
in this book,

cue0143420
00:15:16.599 --> 00:15:20.599
which is a pupil of a pupil of Ha'ari,

cue0143421
00:15:20.599 --> 00:15:25.599
mentions philosophy right and left.

cue0143422
00:15:25.599 --> 00:15:28.599
And he doesn't see any

cue0143423
00:15:28.599 --> 00:15:30.599
embarrassment of prohibition

cue0143424
00:15:30.599 --> 00:15:34.599
into investigating philosophy and mysticism

cue0143425
00:15:34.599 --> 00:15:36.599
together.

cue0143426
00:15:36.599 --> 00:15:38.599
And then he goes on to show

cue0143427
00:15:38.599 --> 00:15:41.599
that another great mystic,

cue0143428
00:15:41.599 --> 00:15:44.599
there were two great mystics at the time of Ha'ari,

cue0143429
00:15:44.599 --> 00:15:46.599
Ha'ari or Isaac de Lourdes,

cue0143430
00:15:46.599 --> 00:15:48.599
and another super, super, one of the greatest,

cue0143431
00:15:48.599 --> 00:15:52.599
Rabbi Moshe Cordovero.

cue0143432
00:15:52.599 --> 00:15:54.599
Now, Ha'ari did not write anything.

cue0143433
00:15:54.599 --> 00:15:56.599
We have not a letter, but we have the pupil,

cue0143434
00:15:56.599 --> 00:15:57.599
Strugo.

cue0143435
00:15:57.599 --> 00:15:59.599
Strugo taught philosophy.

cue0143436
00:15:59.599 --> 00:16:01.599
And we have Cordovero that,

cue0143437
00:16:01.599 --> 00:16:04.599
as Rabbi Arminiento shows,

cue0143438
00:16:04.599 --> 00:16:09.599
quotes philosophers like Plato.

cue0143439
00:16:09.599 --> 00:16:12.599
Which goes to show

cue0143440
00:16:12.599 --> 00:16:14.599
that the whole idea

cue0143441
00:16:14.599 --> 00:16:18.599
that there is a contradiction

cue0143442
00:16:18.599 --> 00:16:23.599
between Tora and science,

cue0143443
00:16:23.599 --> 00:16:26.599
intelligence, logic, rationality,

cue0143444
00:16:26.599 --> 00:16:27.599
it's not true.

cue0143445
00:16:27.599 --> 00:16:29.599
It is not true.

cue0143446
00:16:29.599 --> 00:16:33.599
But it goes more than this.

cue0143447
00:16:33.599 --> 00:16:36.599
The Tora

cue0143448
00:16:36.599 --> 00:16:39.599
describes the people of Israel

cue0143449
00:16:39.599 --> 00:16:42.599
as am-kha-kham,

cue0143450
00:16:42.599 --> 00:16:44.599
ben-avon,

cue0143451
00:16:44.599 --> 00:16:46.599
shabios, wise,

cue0143452
00:16:46.599 --> 00:16:49.599
and intelligent.

cue0143453
00:16:49.599 --> 00:16:51.599
We ask from God,

cue0143454
00:16:51.599 --> 00:16:54.599
v'honenu meidekha Ḥokhma bin avadad.

cue0143455
00:16:54.599 --> 00:16:58.599
Always, knowledge, knowledge, knowledge.

cue0143456
00:16:58.599 --> 00:17:02.599
King Solomon,

cue0143457
00:17:02.599 --> 00:17:04.599
he's blessed with what?

cue0143458
00:17:04.599 --> 00:17:07.599
Wisdom.

cue0143459
00:17:07.599 --> 00:17:09.599
And something very important

cue0143460
00:17:09.599 --> 00:17:12.599
that Rabbi David Nieto mentions.

cue0143461
00:17:12.599 --> 00:17:13.599
Today we know more than that,

cue0143462
00:17:13.599 --> 00:17:15.599
Rabbi David Nieto, about this.

cue0143463
00:17:15.599 --> 00:17:17.599
About 3 or 4 years ago,

cue0143464
00:17:17.599 --> 00:17:19.599
there is a journal,

cue0143465
00:17:19.599 --> 00:17:20.599
I think it's a monthly,

cue0143466
00:17:20.599 --> 00:17:22.599
I used to be a subscriber to it,

cue0143467
00:17:22.599 --> 00:17:24.599
it's called Biblical Archaeology.

cue0143468
00:17:24.599 --> 00:17:25.599
I think it's a monthly.

cue0143469
00:17:25.599 --> 00:17:26.599
I used to receive it.

cue0143470
00:17:26.599 --> 00:17:28.599
I stopped, because I went to Israel,

cue0143471
00:17:28.599 --> 00:17:30.599
I stopped this.

cue0143472
00:17:30.599 --> 00:17:33.599
From the archaeological studies

cue0143473
00:17:33.599 --> 00:17:36.599
of the remnants of the Beth Amikdash.

cue0143474
00:17:36.599 --> 00:17:38.599
And these archaeologists were Christians,

cue0143475
00:17:38.599 --> 00:17:39.599
they are not Jews.

cue0143476
00:17:39.599 --> 00:17:42.599
They came to the following conclusion.

cue0143477
00:17:42.599 --> 00:17:45.599
It was the most important building

cue0143478
00:17:45.599 --> 00:17:47.599
ever built in ancient times.

cue0143479
00:17:47.599 --> 00:17:51.599
No Greek, no Roman building

cue0143480
00:17:51.599 --> 00:17:53.599
could compare to any of the Beth Amikdash.

cue0143481
00:17:53.599 --> 00:17:56.599
And among the miracles of the Beth Amikdash.

cue0143482
00:17:56.599 --> 00:17:57.599
The miracles of the Beth Amikdash,

cue0143483
00:17:57.599 --> 00:17:59.599
I just want to mention one of the things

cue0143484
00:17:59.599 --> 00:18:02.599
that I mentioned in this article,

cue0143485
00:18:02.599 --> 00:18:04.599
in the Biblical Archaeology.

cue0143486
00:18:04.599 --> 00:18:10.599
It served as a perfect astronomical observatory.

cue0143487
00:18:10.599 --> 00:18:13.599
If you are in this gate,

cue0143488
00:18:13.599 --> 00:18:14.599
and you see the sun,

cue0143489
00:18:14.599 --> 00:18:16.599
means that this is, I don't know,

cue0143490
00:18:16.599 --> 00:18:17.599
27 of Elul.

cue0143491
00:18:17.599 --> 00:18:19.599
And if you go on to other days,

cue0143492
00:18:19.599 --> 00:18:21.599
it has to be, I don't know,

cue0143493
00:18:21.599 --> 00:18:23.599
Rishon Be Adar, whatever it is.

cue0143494
00:18:23.599 --> 00:18:26.599
So the Beth Amikdash was built in such a way,

cue0143495
00:18:26.599 --> 00:18:28.599
that if you are a Kohen, of course,

cue0143496
00:18:28.599 --> 00:18:29.599
you know the secrets,

cue0143497
00:18:29.599 --> 00:18:31.599
the insights and outs of it.

cue0143498
00:18:31.599 --> 00:18:33.599
Yes, you could practically know

cue0143499
00:18:33.599 --> 00:18:34.599
exactly the time,

cue0143500
00:18:34.599 --> 00:18:36.599
the most exact time of the season,

cue0143501
00:18:36.599 --> 00:18:39.599
and all about astronomy and so on.

cue0143502
00:18:39.599 --> 00:18:44.599
Obviously, this presupposes

cue0143503
00:18:44.599 --> 00:18:48.599
not only knowledge of science,

cue0143504
00:18:48.599 --> 00:18:51.599
but the desire to teach it,

cue0143505
00:18:51.599 --> 00:18:54.599
and to pass it to others.

cue0143506
00:18:54.599 --> 00:18:57.599
But then comes more than this.

cue0143507
00:18:57.599 --> 00:18:59.599
It's not going to tell you one proof.

cue0143508
00:18:59.599 --> 00:19:02.599
It's going to develop, and develop, and develop.

cue0143509
00:19:02.599 --> 00:19:06.599
And says, well, let's see about science.

cue0143510
00:19:06.599 --> 00:19:09.599
The earliest form of science

cue0143511
00:19:09.599 --> 00:19:12.599
that any civilization has

cue0143512
00:19:12.599 --> 00:19:14.599
is rhetoric.

cue0143513
00:19:14.599 --> 00:19:17.599
Let me explain what rhetoric is.

cue0143514
00:19:17.599 --> 00:19:18.599
Not as we explain it today,

cue0143515
00:19:18.599 --> 00:19:21.599
the way rhetoric today has a negative...

cue0143516
00:19:22.599 --> 00:19:25.599
But in pre-modern times,

cue0143517
00:19:25.599 --> 00:19:27.599
16th, 17th century,

cue0143518
00:19:27.599 --> 00:19:32.599
rhetoric includes what today we call literature,

cue0143519
00:19:32.599 --> 00:19:34.599
how to write,

cue0143520
00:19:34.599 --> 00:19:36.599
prose,

cue0143521
00:19:36.599 --> 00:19:37.599
poetry,

cue0143522
00:19:37.599 --> 00:19:39.599
different kinds of poetry,

cue0143523
00:19:39.599 --> 00:19:43.599
different kinds of writing poetry,

cue0143524
00:19:43.599 --> 00:19:46.599
different ways to interpret the words,

cue0143525
00:19:46.599 --> 00:19:49.599
the art of rhetoric.

cue0143526
00:19:49.599 --> 00:19:51.599
And if you look into, I don't know,

cue0143527
00:19:51.599 --> 00:19:52.599
British encyclopedia,

cue0143528
00:19:52.599 --> 00:19:54.599
Encyclopædia Britannica,

cue0143529
00:19:54.599 --> 00:19:55.599
and their rhetoric,

cue0143530
00:19:55.599 --> 00:19:56.599
they will mention,

cue0143531
00:19:56.599 --> 00:19:58.599
first rhetoric,

cue0143532
00:19:58.599 --> 00:19:59.599
the Greeks,

cue0143533
00:19:59.599 --> 00:20:03.599
around 5th century BC.

cue0143534
00:20:03.599 --> 00:20:06.599
Some of this rhetoric,

cue0143535
00:20:06.599 --> 00:20:09.599
Roman, much later,

cue0143536
00:20:09.599 --> 00:20:12.599
about the 1st century BC.

cue0143537
00:20:12.599 --> 00:20:14.599
But David Avignatus says,

cue0143538
00:20:14.599 --> 00:20:16.599
all this is false.

cue0143539
00:20:16.599 --> 00:20:19.599
The first works

cue0143540
00:20:19.599 --> 00:20:21.599
exhibiting a total, complete,

cue0143541
00:20:21.599 --> 00:20:24.599
absolute knowledge of rhetoric

cue0143542
00:20:24.599 --> 00:20:26.599
is the good book,

cue0143543
00:20:26.599 --> 00:20:28.599
the Bible, the Tora.

cue0143544
00:20:28.599 --> 00:20:31.599
You have there poetry,

cue0143545
00:20:31.599 --> 00:20:34.599
like Shirat Moshe, Shirat Ayam,

cue0143546
00:20:34.599 --> 00:20:36.599
Haʾazinu.

cue0143547
00:20:36.599 --> 00:20:38.599
You have, when Yaʿaqoḇ says,

cue0143548
00:20:38.599 --> 00:20:40.599
Yaʿaqoḇ, for instance, says,

cue0143549
00:20:40.599 --> 00:20:42.599
Achapela panay,

cue0143550
00:20:42.599 --> 00:20:45.599
vaminha haolechet lefanay,

cue0143551
00:20:45.599 --> 00:20:49.599
ulay isa panay,

cue0143552
00:20:49.599 --> 00:20:52.599
vachakach el epanav.

cue0143553
00:20:52.599 --> 00:20:53.599
You have poetry.

cue0143554
00:20:53.599 --> 00:20:55.599
As a matter of fact,

cue0143555
00:20:55.599 --> 00:20:57.599
the first poem

cue0143556
00:20:57.599 --> 00:21:00.599
is said by Adam,

cue0143557
00:21:00.599 --> 00:21:02.599
when he sees Eve.

cue0143558
00:21:02.599 --> 00:21:04.599
Look what he says.

cue0143559
00:21:05.599 --> 00:21:10.599
Lezot yikare isha,

cue0143560
00:21:10.599 --> 00:21:12.599
kime ish lukach azot.

cue0143561
00:21:12.599 --> 00:21:15.599
Ish, isha, zot, zeh.

cue0143562
00:21:15.599 --> 00:21:17.599
Lezot yikare isha,

cue0143563
00:21:17.599 --> 00:21:19.599
kime ish lukach azot.

cue0143564
00:21:19.599 --> 00:21:22.599
You have poetry.

cue0143565
00:21:22.599 --> 00:21:24.599
Then he goes to show,

cue0143566
00:21:24.599 --> 00:21:26.599
for instance, analyzes the poetry

cue0143567
00:21:26.599 --> 00:21:29.599
of Deborah.

cue0143568
00:21:29.599 --> 00:21:32.599
The poetry that you find

cue0143569
00:21:32.599 --> 00:21:34.599
in King David,

cue0143570
00:21:34.599 --> 00:21:35.599
not only in Tehillim,

cue0143571
00:21:35.599 --> 00:21:38.599
but also in Shemuʾel.

cue0143572
00:21:38.599 --> 00:21:40.599
And then goes to show

cue0143573
00:21:40.599 --> 00:21:43.599
all the tricks,

cue0143574
00:21:43.599 --> 00:21:46.599
the literary tricks,

cue0143575
00:21:46.599 --> 00:21:48.599
yes, used by the Bible,

cue0143576
00:21:48.599 --> 00:21:51.599
and it really is one of the most

cue0143577
00:21:51.599 --> 00:21:52.599
magnificent passages,

cue0143578
00:21:52.599 --> 00:21:56.599
I read, of how to understand the Bible

cue0143579
00:21:56.599 --> 00:21:58.599
as a work of rhetoric,

cue0143580
00:21:58.599 --> 00:22:01.599
to understand the use of metaphors,

cue0143581
00:22:01.599 --> 00:22:03.599
the different kind of metaphors that there are,

cue0143582
00:22:03.599 --> 00:22:08.599
all the literary and linguistic skills

cue0143583
00:22:08.599 --> 00:22:10.599
exhibited in the Bible.

cue0143584
00:22:10.599 --> 00:22:13.599
By the way, whenever I read this,

cue0143585
00:22:13.599 --> 00:22:15.599
I'm angry.

cue0143586
00:22:15.599 --> 00:22:18.599
I'm angry because when we teach

cue0143587
00:22:18.599 --> 00:22:20.599
in our schools,

cue0143588
00:22:20.599 --> 00:22:22.599
I am in favor of Jewish education,

cue0143589
00:22:22.599 --> 00:22:23.599
don't take me wrong,

cue0143590
00:22:23.599 --> 00:22:26.599
but I believe,

cue0143591
00:22:26.599 --> 00:22:28.599
and I know many of you are going to disagree,

cue0143592
00:22:29.599 --> 00:22:33.599
that we don't get our money's worth.

cue0143593
00:22:33.599 --> 00:22:34.599
Simply.

cue0143594
00:22:34.599 --> 00:22:36.599
For what the enormous amount of money

cue0143595
00:22:36.599 --> 00:22:38.599
that we as parents pay for school,

cue0143596
00:22:38.599 --> 00:22:40.599
we don't get our money's worth.

cue0143597
00:22:40.599 --> 00:22:41.599
And I said to myself,

cue0143598
00:22:41.599 --> 00:22:45.599
why they cannot teach this to our boys and girls?

cue0143599
00:22:45.599 --> 00:22:47.599
Because when the boys and girls,

cue0143600
00:22:47.599 --> 00:22:49.599
they read Shakespeare, and Milton,

cue0143601
00:22:49.599 --> 00:22:52.599
and other great literary figures

cue0143602
00:22:52.599 --> 00:22:54.599
of the English language,

cue0143603
00:22:54.599 --> 00:22:56.599
they don't know

cue0143604
00:22:56.599 --> 00:23:00.599
that thousands of years before that,

cue0143605
00:23:00.599 --> 00:23:06.599
we were the inventors of literature.

cue0143606
00:23:06.599 --> 00:23:07.599
Let me tell you,

cue0143607
00:23:07.599 --> 00:23:08.599
I want to share with you an anecdote,

cue0143608
00:23:08.599 --> 00:23:10.599
a true anecdote.

cue0143609
00:23:10.599 --> 00:23:12.599
When I was in school,

cue0143610
00:23:12.599 --> 00:23:14.599
in high school,

cue0143611
00:23:14.599 --> 00:23:16.599
in Argentina,

cue0143612
00:23:16.599 --> 00:23:19.599
we had to study Spanish literature.

cue0143613
00:23:19.599 --> 00:23:20.599
And at the time, of course,

cue0143614
00:23:20.599 --> 00:23:21.599
I didn't know,

cue0143615
00:23:21.599 --> 00:23:22.599
how could I know that?

cue0143616
00:23:22.599 --> 00:23:23.599
I didn't know that.

cue0143617
00:23:23.599 --> 00:23:26.599
Friar Louis de Leon was a Jew.

cue0143618
00:23:26.599 --> 00:23:27.599
He was born a Jew,

cue0143619
00:23:27.599 --> 00:23:29.599
and he spent X amount of years in the Inquisition.

cue0143620
00:23:29.599 --> 00:23:31.599
I write about him in my new book.

cue0143621
00:23:31.599 --> 00:23:33.599
I didn't know this.

cue0143622
00:23:33.599 --> 00:23:34.599
And the teacher,

cue0143623
00:23:34.599 --> 00:23:37.599
I remember she was a lady,

cue0143624
00:23:37.599 --> 00:23:41.599
wrote one of his poems.

cue0143625
00:23:41.599 --> 00:23:43.599
And I was, you know, 14, 15,

cue0143626
00:23:43.599 --> 00:23:45.599
between that age.

cue0143627
00:23:45.599 --> 00:23:47.599
And I said to the teacher,

cue0143628
00:23:47.599 --> 00:23:50.599
well, this is like a Hebrew poem.

cue0143629
00:23:50.599 --> 00:23:51.599
It's really so.

cue0143630
00:23:51.599 --> 00:23:52.599
It's much like a Bakashot.

cue0143631
00:23:52.599 --> 00:23:53.599
The same style, you know,

cue0143632
00:23:53.599 --> 00:23:56.599
like the last stroke goes to,

cue0143633
00:23:56.599 --> 00:23:57.599
in every four lines,

cue0143634
00:23:57.599 --> 00:23:59.599
the fourth line of every stroke,

cue0143635
00:23:59.599 --> 00:24:01.599
goes like that.

cue0143636
00:24:01.599 --> 00:24:02.599
And the woman looked at me,

cue0143637
00:24:02.599 --> 00:24:04.599
she didn't know what I was talking about.

cue0143638
00:24:04.599 --> 00:24:06.599
And then I realized,

cue0143639
00:24:06.599 --> 00:24:09.599
that because we sing Bakashot,

cue0143640
00:24:09.599 --> 00:24:11.599
we Jews understood poetry

cue0143641
00:24:11.599 --> 00:24:14.599
better than this lady.

cue0143642
00:24:14.599 --> 00:24:16.599
Now, why can it be,

cue0143643
00:24:16.599 --> 00:24:19.599
that when we study our Tora,

cue0143644
00:24:19.599 --> 00:24:21.599
which is the structure of this literature,

cue0143645
00:24:21.599 --> 00:24:23.599
so that when we read, I don't know,

cue0143646
00:24:23.599 --> 00:24:24.599
Shakespeare, or Milton,

cue0143647
00:24:24.599 --> 00:24:27.599
or whoever it happens to be,

cue0143648
00:24:27.599 --> 00:24:30.599
you know, I don't know how many hours,

cue0143649
00:24:30.599 --> 00:24:32.599
it's at least as good as that.

cue0143650
00:24:32.599 --> 00:24:35.599
So, he goes to show

cue0143651
00:24:35.599 --> 00:24:38.599
the magnificent, magnificent, superb,

cue0143652
00:24:38.599 --> 00:24:40.599
literary styles of people like

cue0143653
00:24:40.599 --> 00:24:44.599
Yeshayah, Yirmiah, Ezekiel.

cue0143654
00:24:44.599 --> 00:24:45.599
And he says,

cue0143655
00:24:45.599 --> 00:24:48.599
in light of this literature,

cue0143656
00:24:48.599 --> 00:24:51.599
the Greeks and the Romans

cue0143657
00:24:51.599 --> 00:24:55.599
are very, very poor imitators.

cue0143658
00:24:55.599 --> 00:24:56.599
Because he says,

cue0143659
00:24:56.599 --> 00:24:59.599
do not forget,

cue0143660
00:24:59.599 --> 00:25:03.599
that the Greek letters

cue0143661
00:25:03.599 --> 00:25:05.599
were taken from the Hebrew.

cue0143662
00:25:05.599 --> 00:25:07.599
He knew that.

cue0143663
00:25:07.599 --> 00:25:08.599
You know what is the first,

cue0143664
00:25:08.599 --> 00:25:11.599
how you say A in Greek?

cue0143665
00:25:11.599 --> 00:25:13.599
Alpha.

cue0143666
00:25:13.599 --> 00:25:15.599
How you say Beth?

cue0143667
00:25:15.599 --> 00:25:16.599
Beta.

cue0143668
00:25:16.599 --> 00:25:18.599
How you say Yod?

cue0143669
00:25:18.599 --> 00:25:19.599
Iota.

cue0143670
00:25:19.599 --> 00:25:21.599
Etc, etc, etc.

cue0143671
00:25:21.599 --> 00:25:23.599
The same order,

cue0143672
00:25:23.599 --> 00:25:25.599
and many of them,

cue0143673
00:25:25.599 --> 00:25:28.599
they have still the same form

cue0143674
00:25:28.599 --> 00:25:31.599
as the Hebrew letters.

cue0143675
00:25:31.599 --> 00:25:35.599
So, the thesis of Rabbi David Nieto

cue0143676
00:25:35.599 --> 00:25:37.599
is an extraordinary thesis.

cue0143677
00:25:37.599 --> 00:25:39.599
He believed like this.

cue0143678
00:25:39.599 --> 00:25:41.599
Historical thing is correct.

cue0143679
00:25:41.599 --> 00:25:45.599
The Greeks were illiterate.

cue0143680
00:25:46.599 --> 00:25:48.599
Today people show,

cue0143681
00:25:48.599 --> 00:25:49.599
the great scholars show,

cue0143682
00:25:49.599 --> 00:25:51.599
that Homer, for instance,

cue0143683
00:25:51.599 --> 00:25:54.599
was recited by heart.

cue0143684
00:25:54.599 --> 00:25:56.599
They did not write Homer.

cue0143685
00:25:56.599 --> 00:26:00.599
He said, but when Alexander

cue0143686
00:26:00.599 --> 00:26:04.599
conquered the Middle East,

cue0143687
00:26:04.599 --> 00:26:08.599
they plundered the library

cue0143688
00:26:08.599 --> 00:26:12.599
of Sholem Aleichem.

cue0143689
00:26:13.599 --> 00:26:16.599
Wisdom that was preserved by us,

cue0143690
00:26:16.599 --> 00:26:20.599
the famous libraries of Sholem Aleichem,

cue0143691
00:26:20.599 --> 00:26:24.599
that were preserved by us for centuries,

cue0143692
00:26:24.599 --> 00:26:28.599
were plundered by the Greeks.

cue0143693
00:26:28.599 --> 00:26:31.599
And they robbed our books,

cue0143694
00:26:31.599 --> 00:26:33.599
and when they came in contact with us

cue0143695
00:26:33.599 --> 00:26:39.599
and discovered the rhetoric, poetry, signs,

cue0143696
00:26:39.599 --> 00:26:41.599
they imitated us.

cue0143697
00:26:41.599 --> 00:26:45.599
So, the first point that he tries to demonstrate

cue0143698
00:26:45.599 --> 00:26:48.599
is the following.

cue0143699
00:26:48.599 --> 00:26:51.599
ʾAbraham ʾAḇinu

cue0143700
00:26:51.599 --> 00:26:54.599
is the father of the scientific methodology.

cue0143701
00:26:54.599 --> 00:26:57.599
Because he believed

cue0143702
00:26:57.599 --> 00:27:02.599
in one single monotheistic God,

cue0143703
00:27:02.599 --> 00:27:07.599
he had to believe that all the universe

cue0143704
00:27:08.599 --> 00:27:12.599
is the result of a single system.

cue0143705
00:27:12.599 --> 00:27:14.599
That there cannot be a contradiction

cue0143706
